@prompt
A list holds a variable number of values of the same type, written `[a, b, c]`. `map` applies a function to each element. You can reference an existing function, or define a new one inline as an *anonymous function* with `fun`:
```hazelnostatics
map([1, 2, 3], fun n -> n * 2)
```

# Tasks

1. Complete `garden` as a list of the four base water amounts: `250`, `180`, `50`, `200`. Once it is filled in, note how the values now appear in the rest of the program.
2. As you finish this next part, pause to watch the values update: give every plant its 50 ml new-moon top-up by mapping over `garden`, adding 50 to each.
3. Click the sample at the end of the `map` line, then press **Space**: you get one sample per element, one for each run of your anonymous function.

@hint
A list is `[a, b, c, d]`. Then an anonymous function `fun w -> ...` that adds 50.

@reference
### Lists
```hazel
[1, 2, 3]
```
A list of integers has type `[Int]`.

### Map and anonymous functions
```hazel
map([1, 2, 3], fun n -> n + 1)
```
`fun n -> ...` is a function with no name.

### More list functions
- `length(xs)` how many elements
- `nth(xs, 0)` the element at an index
- `filter(xs, fun x -> x > 100)` keeps the elements that pass
- `mapi(xs, ...)` is map, but the callback's pair also provides the index

